# Copy to .env and edit. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing here is required. Every number in the README reproduces from
# ./run.sh with none of these set and no network access at all.
#
# Nothing loads .env automatically. There is no python-dotenv dependency, so
# load it yourself before running:
#
#   set -a; source .env; set +a
#   ./run.sh


# --- optional: live LLM review notes (Phase 8) ---------------------------
#
# Without this, explanations fall back to the template writer and the pipeline
# finishes exactly as it does now. All 1,334 committed notes in
# cache/explanations/ are template notes, labelled "source": "template".
#
# With it, `python -m detector.explain --live` calls gpt-oss:120b on Ollama
# Cloud and caches what comes back. Detection is unaffected either way: the
# model turns numbers into a sentence and does no detection.
#
# Get a key at https://ollama.com. Never commit the filled-in .env.
OLLAMA_API_KEY=


# --- optional: how much of the machine to use ----------------------------
#
# run.sh defaults this to 4 if it is unset. It caps the BLAS thread pool, and
# without a cap numpy and scikit-learn each claim every core and the desktop
# stops responding. OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS and MKL_NUM_THREADS follow it.
#
# Memory is handled separately and is not configurable here: every entry point
# calls detector.resources.apply(), which reserves 3 GB for the desktop and
# refuses to run if less than 1.5 GB is left after that.
OMP_NUM_THREADS=4
